// @PLN130 probe 28 — cluster II matrix: invalidator = writing a KEY field through a view.
//
// CONTRACT CHANGED (F4 — fixed by reusing F2's materialise).  Previously `c.key = 5` wrote
// the element's record while the collection kept its old index, so the element ended up
// reachable by NEITHER key while still being iterated:
//
//   c.key == 5   c.tag == 333      the record was updated
//   s[5]  -> null                  not findable at its new key
//   s[30] -> null                  not findable at its old key either
//   iterate -> 3                   still present, so nothing was deleted
//
// Measured identically on `sorted`, `hash` AND `index`, so it was one defect, not three.
//
// A true re-index is the obvious fix and is wrong here: it needs the OLD key captured before
// the write, and for a `sorted` the record MOVES to its new position — invalidating the very
// view doing the writing (F2 all over again, for its own binding).
//
// So a key-field write is now treated as a RESHAPE of that collection: the view materialises
// and the author is told —
//
//   advice: `c` was copied out of `s` because `key` is one of `s`'s keys — writing a key
//   through an element would leave it reachable by no key at all. Writes through `c` no
//   longer reach `s`; to change a key, re-insert with `s[key] = value`.
//
// `c` is now an independent copy, and the collection keeps a consistent, reachable entry.
// Changing a key keeps its supported spelling, `s[key] = value`, which re-indexes properly.

struct Elm { key: integer, tag: integer = 0 }

fn main() {
  s: sorted<Elm[key]> = [Elm { key: 10, tag: 111 },
                         Elm { key: 20, tag: 222 },
                         Elm { key: 30, tag: 333 }];

  c = s[30];
  assert(c.tag == 333, "28 pre c.tag {c.tag} want 333");

  c.key = 5;                       // key write — `c` materialised, `s` untouched

  // The copy took the write.
  assert(c.key == 5, "28 c.key {c.key} want 5");
  assert(c.tag == 333, "28 c.tag {c.tag} want 333");

  // And nothing in the collection became unreachable — the element is still at its own key.
  assert(s[30].tag == 333, "28 s[30].tag {s[30].tag ?? -1} want 333 (must stay reachable)");
  assert(s[10].tag == 111, "28 s[10].tag {s[10].tag ?? -1} want 111");
  assert(s[20].tag == 222, "28 s[20].tag {s[20].tag ?? -1} want 222");

  n = 0;
  for e in s {
    n = n + 1;
  }
  assert(n == 3, "28 iterate {n} want 3");
  println("probe 28 PASSED — key write materialised; no element became unreachable");
}
